# Cursor Rules — Pinterest-to-Obsidian Chrome Extension

## Project Context

Chrome Manifest V3 extension. Vanilla JavaScript, no build step, ES modules. Exports Pinterest boards to Obsidian markdown with images.

## Tech Stack

- Chrome Extension MV3 (service workers)
- Vanilla JS with ES modules
- Chrome APIs: storage, downloads, scripting, tabs, runtime
- No npm, no bundler, no framework

## Architecture

```
content.js (Pinterest DOM) → background.js (service worker) → downloads
                                    ↑
                               popup.js (UI)
```

## Critical Rules

### 1. Service Worker Constraints

Service workers terminate after ~30s. NEVER store state in memory:

```javascript
// ❌ WRONG - state lost on termination
let exportState = { pins: [] };

// ✅ CORRECT - persist to storage
await chrome.storage.local.set({ exportState: { pins: [] } });
```

### 2. Async Message Handlers

ALWAYS return true for async responses:

```javascript
// ❌ WRONG - response never sent
chrome.runtime.onMessage.addListener((msg, sender, sendResponse) => {
  fetchData().then(sendResponse);
});

// ✅ CORRECT
chrome.runtime.onMessage.addListener((msg, sender, sendResponse) => {
  fetchData().then(sendResponse);
  return true; // Keep channel open
});
```

### 3. Content Script Isolation

Content scripts run in Pinterest's context. They CANNOT:
- Import ES modules (must be self-contained)
- Access chrome.downloads directly
- Fetch cross-origin images

Send data to background script instead:
```javascript
chrome.runtime.sendMessage({ action: 'download', url: imageUrl });
```

### 4. No Build Step

Files load directly. When editing:
- Use relative imports: `import { fn } from './utils/file.js'`
- Include .js extension in imports
- Test by refreshing extension in chrome://extensions/

## Code Patterns

### Message Actions
```javascript
// Use verb-noun pattern
{ action: 'extractPins' }
{ action: 'startExport', pins, boardName }
{ action: 'downloadProgress', current, total }
```

### Logging
```javascript
console.log('[Background] ...'); // Service worker
console.log('[Content] ...');    // Content script
console.log('[Popup] ...');      // Popup script
```

### Error Handling
```javascript
try {
  await chrome.downloads.download({ url, filename });
} catch (error) {
  console.error('[Background] Download failed:', error);
  // Handle gracefully, don't crash
}
```

## Pinterest DOM Selectors

```javascript
// These may change - Pinterest updates frequently
'[data-test-id="pinWrapper"]'  // Pin container
'a[href*="/pin/"]'             // Pin link
'img'                          // Inside wrapper

// Image resolution upgrade
url.replace(/\/\d+x\//, '/originals/')
```

## File Responsibilities

| File | Purpose | Can Import Modules |
|------|---------|-------------------|
| background.js | Downloads, storage, coordination | Yes |
| content.js | DOM extraction, scroll | No |
| popup.js | UI logic, settings | Yes |
| utils/*.js | Shared utilities | Yes |

## Testing

1. `chrome://extensions/` → Load unpacked
2. Navigate to Pinterest board
3. Click extension icon
4. Refresh extension after code changes

## Common Pitfalls

| Issue | Solution |
|-------|----------|
| "Cannot use import" in content.js | Move logic to background.js or inline |
| Message response undefined | Add `return true` in listener |
| State lost between actions | Use chrome.storage.local |
| Images fail to download | Use chrome.downloads, not fetch |

## Obsidian Output

Frontmatter must be valid YAML:
```yaml
---
type: pinterest
title: "Title with: colons quoted"
board: "[[Board Name]]"
image: "[[filename.jpg]]"
---
```

Quote strings with special characters: `:`, `#`, `[`, `]`, `'`, `"`
